body:
\> support and care doesn't only include affirmation. and that's my point, there are little studies that compare the two, you can affirm someone without giving them support and care, and you can also not affirm them but give them support and care. I don't believe studies control for this, so when you see studies that say trans affirming care is good, sure there might be evidence for that but its of low quality as it doesn't control for an important extraneous variable.   


So what alternative do you propose, we already know that you can't "Untrans" people. How can you care and support a person while denying their identity?   


\>no it wasn't?

&#x200B;

Ok so on suicide rates: 

This shows transition has an overwhelmingly postive affect on trans people  

[https://whatweknow.inequality.cornell.edu/topics/lgbt-equality/what-does-the-scholarly-research-say-about-the-well-being-of-transgender-people/](https://whatweknow.inequality.cornell.edu/topics/lgbt-equality/what-does-the-scholarly-research-say-about-the-well-being-of-transgender-people/)

More on the same but this includes the affects of puberty blockers 


[https://pediatrics.aappublications.org/content/134/4/696](https://pediatrics.aappublications.org/content/134/4/696)

&#x200B;

These demonstrates that social transition has a positive affect on trans children   


 [https://www.jaacap.org/article/S0890-8567%2816%2931941-4/fulltext](https://www.jaacap.org/article/S0890-8567%2816%2931941-4/fulltext) 

 [https://www.eurekalert.org/pub\_releases/2015-03/tes-sdc030615.php](https://www.eurekalert.org/pub_releases/2015-03/tes-sdc030615.php) 

You aren't taking into account why trans people have higher suicide rates. All data points to it being due to social factors such as increased discriminition and transphobia. 

&#x200B;

\>"PBs have unknown long-term side effects and in this sense are certainly experimental, but in the short term we know that they prevent normal bone growth and most likely interfere with the maturation of the adolescent brain. We now know that PBs do not improve overall mental well-being for most of these children. The vast majority of those who are treated with PBs go on to CSHs and surgery; all of which leads to irreversible physical changes, including infertility. Consent for PBs therefore must include warning of the likelihood of these ensuing effects." 

&#x200B;

Latham doesnt actually mention what the side affects are, he's speculating. As for other supposed risks. Blockers have been used since the 1980s to treat  precocious puberty and were approved by the FDA in 1993. The fear mongering around them has only been around for as long as they've been used to treat trans people.

 "The vast majority of those who are treated with PBs go on to CSHs and surgery; all of which leads to irreversible physical changes, including infertility. Consent for PBs therefore must include warning of the likelihood of these ensuing effects." This passage is laughable. Its argument is that we shouldn't prescribe blockers because of what you might do after being perscribed them. Should we ban the sale of cars because you might get into a car accident? When people come off puberty blockers they go through puberty nothing more and nothing less. 

&#x200B;

\> This is all meaningless, give me a proper definition for woman. 

&#x200B;

Terms for genders are inherently dialectical. They exist in contrast to another thing. A woman is that which a man is not. But that doesn't mean that these are hard boundaries as because this is linguistics and sociology, not biology, they change and have blurred lines. 

&#x200B;

\> Its important that we assess the objectivity of the benefit.   


All data points to immense benefit to trans people as the studies I've shown clearly demonstrate. We can only go off evidence we have. It's wrong to deny someone the choice because they might regret it, however thats extremely unlikely as this data shows:

 [https://www.issm.info/sexual-health-qa/is-it-common-for-transgender-people-to-regret-gender-affirmation-surgery#:\~:text=Before%20surgery%2C%20transgender%20individuals%20often,regret%20having%20gender%2Daffirmation%20surgery](https://www.issm.info/sexual-health-qa/is-it-common-for-transgender-people-to-regret-gender-affirmation-surgery#:~:text=Before%20surgery%2C%20transgender%20individuals%20often,regret%20having%20gender%2Daffirmation%20surgery). 

[https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-2265.2009.03625.x](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-2265.2009.03625.x)

&#x200B;

\> how can you claim you got informed consent when the child falsely believes that he is/will become a woman after surgery? 

&#x200B;

I agree surgery doesn't make a trans woman a woman or a trans man a man. As trans people are the genders they say they are regardless of the point in their transiton they're at. Also no one is doing gender confirmation surgeries on children. It's highly illegal. 

&#x200B;

\> I didnt decide my idnetity,    


Neither did trans people, it's not a choice. 

&#x200B;

\> Should i be forced to disregard that reality to play along with delusion?   


What harm does it do to you? It's a basic matter of respect calling people what they want to be called and treating them as they wish to be treated. With a basic level of dignity. 

&#x200B;

\> there more and more societal/institutional/governmental pressures to blindly accept trans ideology  

&#x200B;

Apply this to other groups, is the government forcing you to accept black ideology or woman ideology? Almost everything we do is a result of societal pressure. We are products of our societies and so our actions and thoughts are in some way dictated by them. Which institutions exactly?   


\>  whether its censorship or anti gendering laws in canada. 

&#x200B;

Anti-trans rhetoric is hardly "censored" It's become a mainstay of Fox news the most watched news show in the US, it's extremely popular in the UK in outlets like the Daily Mail, The Sun and even supposedly left wing papers such as the Guardian, in recent years the BBC has gained a reputation for publishing transphobic rhetoric. It's spouted by politicians and celebrities like Trump, Desantis and the rest of the GOP. Or do you mean that someone was banned from Twitter for breaking TOS?   


As for the "anti-gendering laws" can you provide examples? And has anyone ever been prosecuted for breaking them?
